From 4f98e589ca8b8dfbf2f43790b7808b6fd18891e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hector Osuna <heommbc@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 17:34:15 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] feat(stream): negotiate a second video stream for
 dual-screen clients

A client with two screens can be sent two displays at once rather than one
display and a trackpad. This is the negotiation half: capability, request
parsing and port allocation. The second capture and encode pipeline is not
wired yet, so no host serves a second stream regardless of configuration.

The protocol already had the shape for this and neither end used it. The SDP
reserves x-nv-video[0..3] and only [0] is ever populated; RTSP stream ids
already carry an index, and cmd_setup parsed the type before the '/' and
discarded the rest. So streamid=video/1/0 resolved to the same port as
video/0/0 -- which is why a client must treat that collision as "not
supported": two decoders fed one interleaved bytestream fails as corruption
rather than as a missing feature.

Off by default. dual_display_source is empty unless set, so upgrading changes
nothing. Sunshine cannot create a virtual monitor -- that needs a signed IddCx
driver, which is a dependency and not a component -- so the virtual path
reports unavailable until one is bound, and the whole feature switches itself
off from that single answer. Capturing a real second monitor works today.

See docs/dual_display_protocol.md for the wire contract and
docs/virtual_display.md for what the driver dependency means.

(cherry picked from 5ad68b89)
